{"key":"enterprise/T1690","id":"T1690","name":"Prevent Command History Logging","domain":"enterprise","level":"technique","parent_id":null,"tactics":["defense-impairment"],"platforms":["ESXi","Linux","macOS","Network Devices","Windows"],"environments":["cloud-hosted-endpoint","network-infrastructure","onprem","virtualization"],"environment_basis":"project_taxonomy_from_upstream_platforms","summary":"Adversaries may impair command history logging to hide commands they run on a compromised system. Various command interpreters keep track of the commands users type in their terminal so that users can retrace what they have done. On Linux and macOS, command history is tracked in a file pointed to by the environment variable `HISTFILE`. When a user logs off a system, this information is flushed to a file in the user's home directory called…","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1690","source_stix_id":"attack-pattern--b831f51c-d22f-4724-bbab-60d056bd1150","source_modified":"2026-05-12T15:12:00.709Z","source_object_version":"1.0","telemetry":{"status":"mapped","components":[{"id":"DC0064","name":"Command Execution","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0064","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--685f917a-e95e-4ba0-ade1-c7d354dae6e0","page":"telemetry/DC0064/"},{"id":"DC0032","name":"Process Creation","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0032","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--3d20385b-24ef-40e1-9f56-f39750379077","page":"telemetry/DC0032/"}],"strategies":[{"id":"DET0563","name":"Detection Strategy for Defense Impairment via Prevent Command History Logging across OS platforms.","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0563","stix_id":"x-mitre-detection-strategy--8577b89d-01e2-4423-8657-caff7ed22737","relationship_id":"relationship--e3fdb794-f7c3-4add-858b-bb8f3f79175d"}],"analytics":[{"id":"AN1555","name":"Analytic 1555","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0563#AN1555","guidance":"Detection of environment variable tampering (HISTFILE, HISTCONTROL, HISTFILESIZE) and absence of expected bash history writes. Correlation of unset or zeroed history variables with active shell sessions is indicative of adversarial evasion.","platforms":["Linux"],"strategy_id":"DET0563"},{"id":"AN1556","name":"Analytic 1556","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0563#AN1556","guidance":"Detection of bash/zsh history suppression via HISTFILE/HISTCONTROL manipulation and absence of ~/.bash_history updates. Observing environment variable changes tied to terminal processes is a strong indicator.","platforms":["macOS"],"strategy_id":"DET0563"},{"id":"AN1557","name":"Analytic 1557","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0563#AN1557","guidance":"Detection of PowerShell history suppression using Set-PSReadLineOption with SaveNothing or altered HistorySavePath. Correlating these options with PowerShell usage highlights adversarial evasion attempts.","platforms":["Windows"],"strategy_id":"DET0563"},{"id":"AN1558","name":"Analytic 1558","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0563#AN1558","guidance":"Detection of unset HISTFILE or modified history variables in ESXi shell sessions. Correlation of suspicious shell sessions with no recorded commands despite active usage.","platforms":["ESXi"],"strategy_id":"DET0563"},{"id":"AN1559","name":"Analytic 1559","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0563#AN1559","guidance":"Detection of CLI commands that disable history logging such as 'no logging'. Anomalous lack of new commands in session logs while activity persists is a strong signal.","platforms":["Network Devices"],"strategy_id":"DET0563"}],"log_sources":[{"component_id":"DC0064","component":"Command Execution","name":"auditd:SYSCALL","channel":"execve calls modifying HISTFILE or HISTCONTROL via unset/export","platforms":["Linux"],"analytic_id":"AN1555","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0563#AN1555","validation":"ups